{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>Because YHWH was unable, etc.</b> The meaning is either that He lacks the power, heaven forbid, against the inhabitants of the land — or in the manner of what Scripture says (Psalms 14:1): ״אָמַר נָבָל בְּלִבּוֹ אֵין אֱלֹהִים״ — meaning, that the power that existed beforehand has ceased, heaven forbid, and in this there is a great profanation of the Name." "<b>The people.</b> This statement comes to counter the argument of bringing in the seed of Moses, and says that there would be a profanation of the Name even in this — if He does not bring in this people — as I have written regarding the verse ״וְשָׁמְעוּ מִצְרַיִם״ (Numbers 14:13). And the force of Moses' argument is that even though they spurn him, if He slaughters them in a single moment there would be a great profanation of the Name throughout the entire world. And perhaps this is what Scripture alluded to at the beginning of Moses' words when he said <b>וַיֹּאמֶר מֹשֶׁה אֶל ה׳</b> — meaning, this utterance was for a reason touching upon HaShem Himself, so that His name not be profaned, heaven forbid." "<b>And slaughtered them in the wilderness.</b> The reason he spoke in this idiom is to allude to what would cause the nations to corporealize the matter, heaven forbid, with respect to the God of the universe. And the reason is that when the cause would be, heaven forbid, that He is limited in power, behold it falls into the category of corporealization, heaven forbid."], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :register {:flags {2 ["God"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:09:06.435975434Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}